<h2>The statutory design</h2>
<p>Order XVIII Rule 4(1) CPC states the rule plainly: in every case, the examination-in-chief of a witness shall be on affidavit, and copies shall be supplied to the opposite party. Sub-rules (2) and (3) deal with the stage that remains oral — cross-examination and re-examination — which may be taken either by the court or by a commissioner, whose record is then returned to the court. The court retains control over objections: questions objected to before a commissioner are noted, and admissibility is ruled on by the court at the appropriate stage.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Issues framed; witnesses listed.</strong> The parties file their lists of witnesses; in commercial suits the case management hearing fixes dates for filing affidavits of evidence and for trial.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Affidavit of evidence filed.</strong> Each witness\'s chief examination is filed as a sworn affidavit, exhibiting the documents that witness proves.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Witness produced.</strong> The deponent appears before the court or local commissioner, formally tenders the affidavit, and faces cross-examination.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Record returned; arguments follow.</strong> The recorded evidence, with noted objections, forms the trial record on which final arguments proceed.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the affidavit deserves more care than it usually gets</h2>
<p>Because the affidavit is drafted in chambers rather than spoken in the box, there is a temptation to make it do too much — to argue the case, characterise the opponent\'s conduct, and assert conclusions of law. That drafting style backfires twice over. First, argumentative passages are not evidence and will be discounted. Second, every unnecessary assertion is a fresh target in cross-examination: the witness must defend each sentence of the affidavit as though spoken on oath in the box, including sentences a drafter inserted without checking the witness\'s personal knowledge.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Personal knowledge first</h3><p>Confine each witness to what that witness saw, did, wrote or received. Facts known only through records should be proved by the record-keeper, not by a deponent swearing to hearsay.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Track the pleadings</h3><p>Structure the affidavit issue-wise against the pleaded case. Anything beyond the pleadings is vulnerable; anything pleaded but left unproved is a gap the other side will exploit.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Exhibit with discipline</h3><p>Every document should be identified, connected to the deponent, and exhibited in sequence. In commercial suits, remember that documents not disclosed under Order XI face serious obstacles to use at trial.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Prepare the deponent</h3><p>The witness must know the affidavit thoroughly — including its annexures — before cross-examination. A deponent who cannot support the affidavit\'s contents damages far more than that one document.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The commercial-suit overlay</h2>
<p>In suits governed by the Commercial Courts Act, 2015, the affidavit-evidence regime operates inside a tighter procedural frame. Case management hearings under Order XV-A fix the dates for filing affidavits of evidence and completing cross-examination, and courts are empowered to impose costs for non-compliance with the timetable. The substituted Order XI imposes a duty of disclosure of documents in the party\'s power and possession at the outset, which means the universe of documents an affidavit can exhibit is largely fixed before trial begins. The combined effect is that a commercial trial is substantially won or lost in the preparation of three documents: the plaint or written statement, the disclosure, and the affidavit of evidence.</p>

<h2>Common objections, and how courts handle them</h2>
<table class="law">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Objection</th><th>Usual treatment</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Affidavit travels beyond pleadings</td><td>Offending portions liable to be ignored; no amendment by evidence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Deponent lacks personal knowledge</td><td>Weight reduced or passage discarded; proper witness should have been produced</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Document exhibited but not disclosed or filed</td><td>Objection noted; admissibility decided by the court, with the disclosure rules applied strictly in commercial suits</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Witness not produced for cross-examination</td><td>Affidavit ordinarily excluded from consideration as untested</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Practice note:</strong> treat the affidavit of evidence as testimony, not as a brief. Draft it in the witness\'s own knowledge and voice, anchor every paragraph to a pleading and a document, and prepare the witness for the only part of the exercise that remains oral — the cross-examination. This article is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
